From: Aegir <dev@aegir.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] llvm: getMainExecutable answers argv[0] on Aegir

clang re-executes itself to run cc1, and takes its own path from
`getMainExecutable`. On Aegir there is no /proc to read and no dynamic loader
worth asking -- the cross build defines HAVE_DLOPEN, but dladdr answers a name
`realpath` then rejects, and there is no getprogpath -- so every path in that
function ends in "". An empty answer makes the driver fork "" and exit 0
having written no object, saying nothing (specs/development.md's Phase 3;
specs/clang-on-aegir.md).

The name the launcher started the program with is the one answer available, so
return it up front and again where `dladdr` fails. aegir-clang's main seeds
LLVM's cached answer from its own argv[0] too, but this is the function every
caller reaches.

diff --git a/llvm/lib/Support/Unix/Path.inc b/llvm/lib/Support/Unix/Path.inc
index 6004e007c0c7..041c074a07d8 100644
--- a/llvm/lib/Support/Unix/Path.inc
+++ b/llvm/lib/Support/Unix/Path.inc
@@ -186,6 +186,14 @@ static char *getprogpath(char ret[PATH_MAX], const char *bin) {
 /// GetMainExecutable - Return the path to the main executable, given the
 /// value of argv[0] from program startup.
 std::string getMainExecutable(const char *argv0, void *MainAddr) {
+  // Aegir: the name the launcher started this program with is the only answer available here.
+  // There is no /proc to read, no dynamic loader worth asking (the cross build defines
+  // HAVE_DLOPEN, but dladdr answers a name that realpath then rejects), and no getprogpath --
+  // so every path below ends in "" and this function is asked for clang's own path whenever the
+  // driver re-executes itself to run cc1. An empty answer makes the driver fork "" and exit 0
+  // having written no object (specs/development.md; specs/clang-on-aegir.md's Phase 3).
+  if (argv0 != nullptr && *argv0 != '\0')
+    return std::string(argv0);
 #if defined(__APPLE__)
   // On OS X the executable path is saved to the stack by dyld. Reading it
   // from there is much faster than calling dladdr, especially for large
@@ -321,8 +329,16 @@ std::string getMainExecutable(const char *argv0, void *MainAddr) {
   // Use dladdr to get executable path if available.
   Dl_info DLInfo;
   int err = dladdr(MainAddr, &DLInfo);
-  if (err == 0)
+  if (err == 0) {
+    // Aegir: the cross build defines HAVE_DLOPEN, but there is no dynamic loader to ask and no
+    // /proc to read, so dladdr answers nothing and the one path available is the name the
+    // launcher started this program with. clang re-executes itself to run cc1 and takes that
+    // path from here: an empty answer makes the driver fork "" -- it exits 0 having written no
+    // object and says nothing (specs/development.md; specs/clang-on-aegir.md's Phase 3).
+    if (argv0 != nullptr && *argv0 != '\0')
+      return std::string(argv0);
     return "";
+  }
 
   // If the filename is a symlink, we need to resolve and return the location of
   // the actual executable.
